        <groundspeak:log id="684927120">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-05-08T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="3854161">Stonagal</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP701 - Fort Saskatchewan.

9:15 pm MDT. Okay for real this is my last one. And perhaps fittingly so. I moved to Alberta in October 2015. Once it was clear we would be in Vegreville I checked to see what virtual caches were in the Alberta Capital Region. This one came up, as it was the 'host' cap at the time. It was my first glimpse of this multi-virtual thing we have going on here.

As for this evening, my first attempt was blocked by fire trucks and rerouted traffic. Later this evening the coast was clear.

Cheers once again!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="680229828">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-04-16T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="7338377">j0ckser</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BCP701 Fort Saskatchewan

Posted: N 53° 42.263 W 113° 12.920

My GPSr: N 53° 42.264 W 113° 12.923&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GeoGuider and I went out to Fort Saskatchewan this morning. It was sunny, but there’s still a lot of snow on the ground from the recent slush storms we’ve had. The cap is in poor condition - the numbers are barely readable. Thanks for the cap outforthehunt.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="679341762">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-04-16T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1095439">GeoGuider</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BCP701 Fort Saskatchewan&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Posted: N 53° 42.263 W 113° 12.920&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;strong&gt;My GPSr: N 53° 42.264 W 113° 12.923&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We had some time before Easter dinner, so j0ckser and I went out to Fort Saskatchewan this morning.  It was sunny, but there’s still a lot of snow on the ground from the recent slush storms we’ve had. This is a first for me.  I’ve never gone looking for brass caps in the snow before.  With the deadline looming, there’s always a first time.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="650401242">
          <groundspeak:date>2016-11-08T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="578246">BVPete</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BCP701 Ft Saskatchewan&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There was not much activity around the cap location today so I was able to park almost beside it. The cap hole was still visible and it only required a bit of a clean up before was able to take my photos. After that we went and got some gas before heading across the river for an unsuccessful attempt at finding the RR Museum cap.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for the Ft Saskatchewan cap ofth, I think this was only my second visit to the town.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
      </groundspeak:logs>
